 we can encourage other  countries to follow along but there's  less risk than there was do you agree  with that I do agree with that but the  question is this I don't want the  American people to think that we have  solved too big to fail when I believe in  my heart we have not solved it yet and  that's why I'm standing up and speaking  forward because if I were standing up I  wouldn't be doing my job I guess what I  want to understand though it was the  Treasury Department Hank Paulson's  Treasury Department that during the  crisis push these takeovers wamu Bear  Stearns those are the contributors to  making these banks even bigger today  that's absolutely true and I was in the  middle of that so I'm not absolving  myself of responsibility I take  responsibility for that in the midst of  that terrible crisis we had to make the  problem worse by encouraging these  mergers just to stabilize the system we  knew at the time hey if we can stabilize  the system that's going to be a  short-term victory we're still going to  need to deal with too big to fail  long-term I just don't want to have the  future Treasury secretary the future Fed  chair 20 years from now or 50 years from  now back in the situation we were in  having to make those terrible choices  then is it fair that the populace  opinion is  still so anti banks and banks continue  to pay fine after fine tied to acquiring  these other banks when it was the  Treasury Department that pushed you to  do it look I understand those concerns  and those are very fair but I also  understand the concerns on Main Street  right the main street was devastated by  this people it's not just people who  bought homes and bought mortgages maybe  they couldn't afford when this  manifested itself into a deep recession  a great recession people lost their jobs  and that wasn't fair and so there's a  lot of anger in the country which I am a  double zero interest rates has hurt Main  Street zero interest rate has helped  banks and hedge funds and money managers  asset holders as their assets have risen  over the last five years and mom and  pops on the street haven't gotten their  wages up Obamacare has made it difficult  for them to hire more people into small  businesses I I agree that asset  valuations have been helping the wealthy  more than they've been helping  middle-class and lower-income agree a  hundred percent and that's what's so  tough about coming out of this terrible  crisis you you all know the Rogoff and  Reinhart book about this time is  different and how hard it is to recover  from these banking crises that's exactly  why we don't want this to happen again  because it ends up not just being Wall  Street who's heard it ends up being Main  Street and they're paying for a year  after year after year so I think you're  making my point for me
 

